# Spec-driven workflow

`openspec/specs/` is the source of truth for how the system behaves. `openspec/changes/<name>/` holds proposed work. `docs/technical-plan.md` holds the engineering behind the specs. `docs/decisions.md` is the dated decision ledger.

## Before changing behavior

- Any change to observable behavior starts as an OpenSpec change (`openspec new change <kebab-name>`), with proposal, delta specs, design (when the change is cross-cutting) and tasks. Do not implement from chat alone.
- Read the relevant main spec in full before writing a delta. Use `MODIFIED` only with the entire requirement block copied; use `ADDED` for new concerns.
- Pure refactors, tooling and docs changes with no behavior change set `skip_specs: true` in the change's `.openspec.yaml`.
- Run `openspec validate <name> --strict` before starting implementation.

## While implementing

- Work from `tasks.md`. Tick a task (`- [x]`) only when its stated verification has actually been run.
- When implementation reveals the spec is wrong, update the delta spec in the same change and say so in the commit; do not silently diverge.
- Keep `docs/technical-plan.md` in step with the code when an engineering approach changes.

## When finishing

- Archive with `openspec archive <name>` (or `/opsx-archive`) so delta specs merge into `openspec/specs/`.
- Append any new or reversed decision to `docs/decisions.md` with date, rationale and status in the same commit as the archive.

## Verification artifacts

- Anything written only to verify work (scratch scripts, sample output) lives outside the repo (for example `/tmp`) or under the ignored `scratch/` directory. Never commit sample customer photos.
